---
import Cite from '../../components/Cite.astro';
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import { codexPage } from '../../data/pages.js';
import { powerOfTwoNotes } from '../../lib/federation.js';

const page = codexPage('power-of-two-notes');
const examples = [13, 45, 100, 1000].map((amount) => ({ amount, notes: powerOfTwoNotes(amount), binary: amount.toString(2) }));
---
<Article slug={page.slug}>
  <p>
    Every ecash note has a fixed value. Fedimint’s mint uses powers of two: 1, 2, 4, 8 and so on, in millisatoshis, up to a configured
    maximum.<Cite page={page} s="fmTiered" /><Cite page={page} s="fmDenominations" /> Each size has its own signing key, which is how a
    note proves its value without the mint having seen it.<Cite page={page} s="fmTbs" />
  </p>

  <h2>Any amount, few notes</h2>
  <p>
    Every whole number is a sum of distinct powers of two, and that sum is just the number written in binary. Each 1 digit is a note.
    An amount below 2<sup>k</sup> never needs more than <em>k</em> notes.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Amounts split into power-of-two notes</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Amount</th><th scope="col">Binary</th><th scope="col">Notes</th><th scope="col">Count</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {examples.map((e) => <tr><th scope="row">{e.amount.toLocaleString('en')}</th><td><code>{e.binary}</code></td><td>{e.notes.join(' + ')}</td><td>{e.notes.length}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Real wallets usually keep a few notes of each size, so they can pay most amounts straight away. When they need change, they trade
    notes with the federation for new ones, and each new note is blinded again.
  </p>

  <h2>Why standard sizes help privacy</h2>
  <p>
    If anyone could ask for a note worth 4,173.5 sats, that note would be easy to spot when it was spent. With a short list of standard
    sizes, a note of 64 looks like every other note of 64, so a spent note blends in with all the others of its size.
  </p>
</Article>
